# Study notes: meditation texts, protection discourses, and Visuddhimagga chapters.
from lay2 import S

MED = {
# ---------------- mettā ----------------
'snp1.8': S('The Discourse on Loving-kindness', 'Karaṇīyamettā Sutta · කරණීය මෙත්ත සූත්‍රය', 'The Sutta Nipāta and the Khuddakapāṭha. The commentary tells of monks who went to a forest for the rains and were frightened by the tree deities; the Buddha sent them back with this discourse.',
  'The best-known text of the Theravāda world. It opens with the qualities of one who wants to reach peace: capable, upright, gentle, humble, content, easy to support, of few duties, light in living, calm in the senses. It then pours out the wish: may all beings be happy, seen and unseen, near and far, born and to be born. As a mother would protect her only child with her life, so one should cultivate a boundless heart toward all beings.',
  ['**The foundation:** fifteen qualities of conduct before the meditation begins.',
   '**The wish:** *sukhino vā khemino hontu, sabbe sattā bhavantu sukhitattā*.',
   '**The method:** pervading beings of every size and kind, then every direction, without obstruction, hatred or enmity.',
   '**The posture:** standing, walking, sitting or lying down, as long as one is awake: this is called the divine abiding here.',
   '**The end:** not grasping at views, virtuous, with vision, free of sensual greed, one does not come again to a womb.'],
  ['mettā', 'meditation', 'paritta'], ['an11.15', 'an9.18', 'mn21']),
'an11.15': S('The Eleven Benefits of Loving-kindness', 'Mettānisaṃsa Sutta', 'Sāvatthī.',
  'When the heart\'s release by loving-kindness is cultivated, developed, made a vehicle and a basis, eleven benefits follow.',
  ['Sleep at ease; wake at ease; no bad dreams.', 'Dear to humans; dear to non-humans; protected by deities.',
   'Fire, poison and weapons do not harm you.', 'The mind quickly becomes concentrated; the face is serene.',
   'You die unconfused; and if you go no further, you are reborn in the Brahmā world.'],
  ['mettā', 'benefits'], ['an8.1', 'snp1.8']),
'an8.1': S('Eight Benefits of Loving-kindness', 'Mettā Sutta', 'Sāvatthī.',
  'The eight-fold version of the benefits, with verses: one who develops mettā even for a single being becomes good; for all beings, a noble one makes abundant merit. Kings who conquered the earth with great sacrifices are not worth a sixteenth part of a heart well developed in love.',
  ['Sleep and wake at ease, no bad dreams, dear to humans and non-humans, protected by deities, safe from fire, poison and weapons, Brahmā world.',
   '"Not worth a sixteenth part of a mind well developed in love."'],
  ['mettā', 'benefits'], ['an11.15', 'iti27']),
'sn20.3': S('Families', 'Kula Sutta', 'Sāvatthī.',
  'Families with many women and few men are easily attacked by bandits. A meditator who does not develop loving-kindness is easily attacked by non-human beings. Families with few women and many men are hard to attack; so is one who develops mettā.',
  ['Mettā as protection.'], ['mettā', 'protection'], ['an11.15', 'an4.67']),
'iti27': S('Loving-kindness Outshines All Merit', 'Mettābhāvanā Sutta · Mettācetovimutti Sutta', 'Itivuttaka, the Book of Ones.',
  'All the grounds for making merit are not worth a sixteenth part of the heart\'s release by loving-kindness. It outshines them, as moonlight outshines the stars, as the autumn sun outshines the sky, as the morning star blazes at dawn.',
  ['Mettā is the brightest merit.', 'Three similes of light.'], ['mettā', 'merit'], ['an8.1', 'an7.62']),
'an4.125': S('Loving-kindness and Rebirth', 'Paṭhamamettā Sutta', 'Taught to the mendicants.',
  'Someone who dwells pervading the world with loving-kindness, compassion, gladness and equanimity enjoys it and may be reborn among the gods of the Brahmā realms, for an aeon or more. An ordinary person, when that life ends, may fall to lower realms; a disciple of the Buddha is fully extinguished in that very existence.',
  ['The divine abodes lead to the Brahmā realms.', 'Insight makes the difference between temporary and final peace.'],
  ['brahmavihāra', 'rebirth'], ['dn13', 'mn7']),
'mn7': S('The Simile of the Cloth', 'Vattha Sutta · Vatthūpama Sutta', 'Sāvatthī.',
  'A dirty cloth will not take the dye; a clean one will. So a defiled mind leads to a bad destination, a clean mind to a good one. The Buddha lists sixteen defilements, from greed to negligence. Purified, with confidence in the Triple Gem, joy arises, then the four divine abodes. To the brahmin Sundarika, who believes in sacred rivers, he says: bathe here, in kindness to all beings.',
  ['Sixteen defilements of mind (*upakkilesa*).', 'Confidence in the Triple Gem as a source of joy.', 'The four divine abodes.', '"The inner bathing."'],
  ['mind', 'brahmavihāra', 'ritual'], ['an4.125', 'an10.176']),
'dn13': S('The Three Knowledges', 'Tevijja Sutta', 'Manasākaṭa, Kosala. The young brahmins Vāseṭṭha and Bhāradvāja argue about which teacher shows the true path to Brahmā.',
  'The Buddha asks: has any of these teachers, or their teachers back to the seventh generation, seen Brahmā? No. Then it is a line of blind men holding each other, a staircase built to a palace no one has seen, a man calling on the far bank of a river to come over. The way to companionship with Brahmā is the gradual path: virtue, abandoning the hindrances, and the four divine abodes, pervading the world "as a strong conch blower is heard in all four directions".',
  ['Authority without experience is empty.', 'The brahmavihāras as the path to Brahmā.', 'The conch-blower simile.'],
  ['brahmavihāra', 'inquiry', 'meditation'], ['an3.65', 'sn42.8']),
# ---------------- breath ----------------
'mn118': S('Mindfulness of Breathing', 'Ānāpānassati Sutta · ආනාපානසති සූත්‍රය', 'Sāvatthī, the Eastern Monastery, the full-moon night of the Pavāraṇā. The Buddha is so pleased with the monks\' practice that he extends the retreat to the Komudī full moon of Kattika.',
  'The fullest teaching on breath meditation: **sixteen steps in four sets of four**, mapped onto the four establishments of mindfulness, which fulfil the seven factors of awakening, which fulfil knowledge and freedom.',
  ['**Body:** long breath, short breath, experiencing the whole body, calming the bodily formation.',
   '**Feeling:** experiencing rapture, happiness, the mental formation, calming it.',
   '**Mind:** experiencing the mind, gladdening it, concentrating it, freeing it.',
   '**Dhammas:** contemplating impermanence, fading, cessation, letting go.',
   'Breath → four satipaṭṭhāna → seven bojjhaṅga → knowledge and freedom.'],
  ['ānāpānasati', 'meditation', 'mindfulness'], ['sn54.1', 'sn54.13', 'mn62']),
'sn54.1': S('One Thing', 'Ekadhamma Sutta', 'Sāvatthī.',
  'One thing, developed and cultivated, is of great fruit: mindfulness of breathing. Here is the method in its simplest canonical form. Go to a forest, the root of a tree or an empty hut; sit cross-legged, body straight; establish mindfulness in front of you; mindful, breathe in, mindful, breathe out. Then the sixteen steps.',
  ['The setting, the posture, the attention.', 'The sixteen steps in brief.'],
  ['ānāpānasati', 'meditation'], ['mn118']),
'sn54.13': S('Ānanda on the Breath', 'Ānanda Sutta', 'Sāvatthī. Ānanda asks whether one thing fulfils four, four fulfil seven, and seven fulfil two.',
  'Yes: **mindfulness of breathing** fulfils the **four establishments of mindfulness**; these fulfil the **seven factors of awakening**; these fulfil **knowledge and freedom**. The awakening factors are developed in dependence on seclusion, dispassion and cessation, ripening in letting go.',
  ['1 → 4 → 7 → 2: the architecture of the path in breath.'], ['ānāpānasati', 'awakening factors'], ['mn118', 'sn46.14']),
'mn62': S('The Longer Advice to Rāhula', 'Mahārāhulovāda Sutta', 'Sāvatthī. The young Rāhula, walking for alms behind the Buddha, admires his own body; the Buddha turns and tells him to see all form as not-self.',
  'Sāriputta later tells Rāhula to practise breath meditation. When Rāhula asks how, the Buddha first teaches the five elements as not-self, then **meditation like the earth** (which is not troubled by what is thrown on it), water, fire, air and space; then the four divine abodes, foulness and impermanence; and finally the sixteen steps of breathing, ending: "even the final breaths are known as they cease."',
  ['The elements meditation as preparation.', 'Meditate like the earth.', 'A complete course in one sutta.'],
  ['ānāpānasati', 'elements', 'brahmavihāra'], ['mn118', 'an10.60']),
'an10.60': S('Girimānanda', 'Girimānanda Sutta · ගිරිමානන්ද සූත්‍රය', 'Sāvatthī. Venerable Girimānanda is gravely ill; the Buddha sends Ānanda with ten perceptions to recite.',
  'Ten perceptions: impermanence, not-self, foulness, drawbacks (a long list of diseases the body is subject to), giving up, fading away, cessation, disenchantment with the whole world, impermanence of all conditions, and **mindfulness of breathing** with its sixteen steps. Hearing them, Girimānanda recovers. It is chanted as pirith for the sick.',
  ['Ten perceptions (*dasa saññā*).', 'The body\'s many illnesses, named.', 'A complete breath meditation within a healing chant.'],
  ['illness', 'paritta', 'ānāpānasati', 'meditation'], ['sn46.14', 'mn118', 'sn55.54']),
# ---------------- body ----------------
'mn10': S('The Establishments of Mindfulness', 'Satipaṭṭhāna Sutta · සතිපට්ඨාන සූත්‍රය', 'Among the Kurus, at Kammāsadhamma.',
  '"This is the direct path for the purification of beings": the four establishments of mindfulness. Observing the body in the body, feelings in feelings, mind in mind and dhammas in dhammas, ardent, aware and mindful, free of desire and aversion for the world. Practised for seven years, or even seven days, it leads to awakening or non-return.',
  ['**Body:** breathing, postures, full awareness in activities, thirty-one parts, four elements, nine charnel-ground contemplations.',
   '**Feelings:** pleasant, painful, neutral; worldly and unworldly.',
   '**Mind:** with or without greed, hatred, delusion; contracted, expanded, concentrated, freed.',
   '**Dhammas:** hindrances, aggregates, sense bases, awakening factors, four noble truths.',
   'Internally, externally, both; arising and passing; "mindfulness established just enough for knowledge."'],
  ['mindfulness', 'meditation', 'satipaṭṭhāna'], ['mn119', 'mn118', 'sn47.20']),
'mn119': S('Mindfulness of the Body', 'Kāyagatāsati Sutta', 'Sāvatthī. The monks are talking about how wonderful the Buddha\'s teaching on body mindfulness is.',
  'The body contemplations of MN 10, followed by the four jhānas with their similes, all as "mindfulness of the body". Whoever develops it includes all skilful qualities, as one who imagines the ocean includes all the rivers that flow into it. Ten benefits follow, from mastering discontent and fear to the ending of the defilements.',
  ['Body practices as a gateway to jhāna.', 'Similes: a heavy stone ball on wet clay (Māra finds a way in) and a ball of string on a wooden board (he does not).', 'Ten benefits.'],
  ['mindfulness', 'body', 'jhāna'], ['mn10', 'snp1.11']),
'snp1.11': S('Victory', 'Vijaya Sutta · Kāyavicchandanika Sutta', 'The Sutta Nipāta. The commentary says it was taught to the nun Rūpanandā and others who prided themselves on beauty.',
  'Walking, standing, sitting or lying down, the body bends and stretches. It is bound together by bones and sinews, smeared with skin and flesh, and filled with things the fool regards as beautiful. Its nine openings ooze constantly. And when it lies dead, swollen, livid, cast away in a charnel ground, dogs and jackals feed on it. "As this is, so is that": the wise see their own body in the dead one.',
  ['Contemplation of the body\'s parts and of death.', '"As this is, so is that; as that is, so is this."', 'The cure for vanity and lust.'],
  ['asubha', 'body', 'meditation'], ['mn119', 'mn10']),
'sn47.19': S('The Acrobat', 'Sedaka Sutta', 'Among the Sumbhas, at Sedaka.',
  'An acrobat tells his apprentice: you watch me and I will watch you, and we will come down safely. The apprentice says: you look after yourself, and I will look after myself. The Buddha sides with the apprentice. **Looking after yourself, you look after others; looking after others, you look after yourself.** The first is mindfulness; the second is patience, harmlessness, love and kindness.',
  ['Mindfulness protects oneself and others.', 'Kindness protects others and oneself.'],
  ['mindfulness', 'relationships'], ['sn47.20', 'mn10']),
'sn47.20': S('The Most Beautiful Girl in the Land', 'Janapadakalyāṇī Sutta', 'Among the Sumbhas, at Sedaka.',
  'A huge crowd gathers to watch the finest dancer and singer in the country. A man is told to carry a bowl brimful of oil between the crowd and the performer, with a man behind him holding a raised sword, ready to cut off his head if a single drop spills. Would he become heedless? The bowl is **mindfulness of the body**.',
  ['A simile for undistracted attention amid temptation.'], ['mindfulness', 'body'], ['sn47.19', 'mn10']),
# ---------------- recollections ----------------
'an6.10': S('The Six Recollections', 'Mahānāma Sutta', 'Kapilavatthu. Mahānāma asks how a noble disciple who has understood the teaching mostly dwells.',
  'They recollect **the Buddha** (*iti pi so bhagavā…*), **the Dhamma** (*svākkhāto…*), **the Saṅgha** (*supaṭipanno…*), **their own virtue**, **their own generosity**, and **the deities**: "the faith, virtue, learning, generosity and wisdom that brought them there, I have too." When they do, the mind is not overcome by greed, hatred or delusion; it is straight, and from it come joy, rapture, tranquillity, happiness and concentration.',
  ['The full formulas of the three recollections of the Triple Gem.', 'Recollecting one\'s own virtue and generosity as a meditation.', 'The sequence: gladness → rapture → tranquillity → happiness → samādhi.'],
  ['buddhānussati', 'recollection', 'meditation'], ['an11.11', 'sn11.3', 'an3.70']),
'an11.11': S('Mahānāma (1)', 'Mahānāma Sutta', 'Kapilavatthu, at the end of the rains. Mahānāma learns the Buddha will soon set out and asks how he should live while apart.',
  'Five qualities to rely on (faith, energy, mindfulness, concentration, wisdom) and then the six recollections, each described in full with its fruit: a straight mind, joy, rapture, tranquillity, happiness and concentration.',
  ['Practise these while walking, standing, sitting, lying down, at work, and at home with a house full of children.'],
  ['recollection', 'lay life', 'meditation'], ['an6.10', 'an11.12']),
'an11.12': S('Mahānāma (2)', 'Dutiyamahānāma Sutta', 'Kapilavatthu.',
  'The same question and teaching, with the explicit instruction that these recollections are to be developed in every posture, while busy at work, and while lying in a bed crowded with children: the canon\'s clearest statement that this is a householder\'s meditation.',
  ['A practice for busy lay life.'], ['recollection', 'lay life'], ['an11.11']),
'sn11.3': S('The Crest of the Banner', 'Dhajagga Sutta · ධජග්ග සූත්‍රය', 'Sāvatthī. The Buddha tells of the war between gods and titans: Sakka told his troops that if they felt fear, they should look up at his banner.',
  'If you are afraid in a forest, at the root of a tree or in an empty hut, recollect the Buddha; or the Dhamma; or the Saṅgha. Fear and terror will vanish. Sakka\'s banner may fail, because Sakka is not free of greed, hatred and delusion; the Buddha is.',
  ['The three recollections as a remedy for fear.', 'A principal paritta of the Sri Lankan tradition.'],
  ['paritta', 'fear', 'buddhānussati'], ['an6.10', 'kp6']),
'an6.19': S('Mindfulness of Death (1)', 'Paṭhamamaraṇassati Sutta', 'Nādika, the brick hall.',
  'The Buddha asks the monks how they practise mindfulness of death. "If I could live a day and night…", "a day…", "for one meal…", "for four or five mouthfuls…". Only those who say "for one mouthful" and "for a single in-and-out breath" practise it diligently. The others are negligent.',
  ['Measure life in breaths, not years.', 'Urgency without fear.'], ['maraṇasati', 'urgency'], ['an6.20', 'an8.73']),
'an6.20': S('Mindfulness of Death (2)', 'Dutiyamaraṇassati Sutta', 'Nādika.',
  'At nightfall and at daybreak, reflect: many things could cause my death: a snake, a scorpion, a centipede, a fall, bad food, bile, phlegm, sharp winds within, humans, non-humans. Do I have bad qualities not yet given up? If so, make an extraordinary effort, like someone whose turban is on fire. If not, live with joy and gladness, training day and night.',
  ['The evening and morning reflection.', 'The turban on fire.', 'Joy when one finds nothing left undone.'],
  ['maraṇasati', 'reflection'], ['an6.19', 'an5.57']),
'an8.73': S('Mindfulness of Death (3)', 'Paṭhamamaraṇassati Sutta', 'Nādika.', 'The same teaching as AN 6.19, in the Book of Eights.',
  ['Only "one mouthful" and "one breath" are diligent.'], ['maraṇasati'], ['an6.19']),
'an8.74': S('Mindfulness of Death (4)', 'Dutiyamaraṇassati Sutta', 'Nādika.', 'The same teaching as AN 6.20, in the Book of Eights.',
  ['Night and morning reflection on the many causes of death.'], ['maraṇasati'], ['an6.20']),
# ---------------- concentration ----------------
'an10.25': S('The Kasiṇas', 'Kasiṇa Sutta', 'Taught to the mendicants.',
  'Ten kasiṇas, "totalities" or universal fields of meditation: **earth, water, fire, air, blue, yellow, red, white, space and consciousness**, each perceived above, below, across, undivided and limitless. This short list is the canonical root of the kasiṇa method that the Visuddhimagga develops at length.',
  ['The ten kasiṇas.', '"Above, below, across, non-dual, measureless."'], ['kasiṇa', 'meditation'], ['vm4', 'vm5', 'an10.26', 'mn77']),
'an10.26': S('Kāḷī', 'Kāḷī Sutta', 'Avantī, Kuraraghara. The laywoman Kāḷī asks Mahākaccāna about a verse the Buddha spoke.',
  'Mahākaccāna explains it through the kasiṇas: some ascetics regard the attainment of the earth kasiṇa (and the others) as the ultimate, and create existence there. The Buddha saw where each one began and ended, its gratification, drawback and escape, and so reached the goal beyond them.',
  ['Kasiṇa attainment is not the end.', 'A laywoman asks and receives an advanced teaching.'],
  ['kasiṇa', 'insight', 'lay life'], ['an10.25']),
'mn77': S('The Longer Discourse with Sakuludāyī', 'Mahāsakuludāyi Sutta', 'Rājagaha. The wanderer Sakuludāyī says the Buddha\'s disciples revere him above other teachers.',
  'The Buddha says it is not for his frugality that they revere him. It is for five things, the last being the practices he has taught: the establishments of mindfulness, right efforts, bases of power, faculties, powers, awakening factors, the eightfold path, the eight liberations, the eight dimensions of mastery, **the ten kasiṇas**, the four jhānas with their similes, insight, the mind-made body, and the higher knowledges. A complete catalogue of meditation.',
  ['The fullest list of practices in the Majjhima.', 'The ten kasiṇas and the dimensions of mastery (*abhibhāyatana*).'],
  ['kasiṇa', 'meditation', 'jhāna'], ['an10.25', 'an5.28']),
'an5.28': S('Right Concentration with Five Factors', 'Pañcaṅgika Sutta · Pañcaṅgikasamādhi Sutta', 'Taught to the mendicants.',
  'The four jhānas, each with its simile: a bath attendant kneading soap powder until moisture fills it; a lake fed by a spring from within; lotuses growing submerged in cool water; a man wrapped from head to foot in a clean white cloth. The fifth factor is the reviewing sign. Then six direct knowledges, each with a simile of its own.',
  ['The jhāna similes, the clearest map of samādhi in the canon.'], ['jhāna', 'samādhi'], ['dn2', 'mn119']),
'an5.29': S('Walking Meditation', 'Caṅkama Sutta · Caṅkamānisaṃsa Sutta', 'Taught to the mendicants.',
  'Five benefits of walking meditation: you can **travel long distances**, you can **sustain striving**, you are **healthy**, **what you eat and drink is well digested**, and **concentration gained while walking lasts long**.',
  ['Five benefits of caṅkama.'], ['walking', 'meditation', 'health'], ['an7.61', 'mn10']),
'mn20': S('Removing Distracting Thoughts', 'Vitakkasaṇṭhāna Sutta', 'Sāvatthī.',
  'Five methods, in order, for when unwholesome thoughts of desire, hatred or delusion keep arising: (1) replace it with a wholesome thought, as a carpenter knocks out a coarse peg with a fine one; (2) examine its drawbacks, as a young person would be horrified by a carcass hung round their neck; (3) stop paying attention to it, as one closes the eyes or looks away; (4) calm its source, as a man walking fast slows down, stands, sits, then lies; (5) with teeth clenched, crush the mind with the mind. Then one is "master of the ways of thought".',
  ['Substitute, examine, ignore, still, restrain.', '"He will think what he wants to think."'],
  ['thoughts', 'meditation', 'mind'], ['mn19', 'an5.161']),
'mn19': S('Two Kinds of Thought', 'Dvedhāvitakka Sutta', 'Sāvatthī. The Buddha recalls his practice before the awakening.',
  'He divided his thoughts into two sides: thoughts of sensuality, ill will and cruelty on one, renunciation, goodwill and harmlessness on the other. **Whatever one frequently thinks and ponders becomes the inclination of the mind.** Like a cowherd who must beat the cows away from the ripe crops in autumn, but after the harvest only needs to be mindful, "there are the cows".',
  ['Sorting thought into two classes.', 'The mind inclines to what it dwells on.', 'From effort to mere mindfulness.'],
  ['thoughts', 'mind', 'meditation'], ['mn20']),
'sn46.55': S('Saṅgārava', 'Saṅgārava Sutta', 'Sāvatthī. The brahmin Saṅgārava asks why texts he has learnt sometimes will not come to mind.',
  'Because the mind is obsessed by a hindrance, and one cannot see one\'s own good, like trying to see one\'s face in water that is **dyed** (sensual desire), **boiling** (ill will), **covered in moss** (dullness), **rippled by wind** (restlessness), or **muddy and in darkness** (doubt). Clear water free of these shows the face; so does the mind with the awakening factors.',
  ['The five bowls of water: the best-known similes for the hindrances.'], ['hindrances', 'meditation', 'memory'], ['an7.61', 'sn54.13']),
'an7.61': S('Nodding Off', 'Pacalā Sutta · Pacalāyamāna Sutta', 'Among the Bhaggas. Moggallāna, a week before his awakening, is fighting drowsiness; the Buddha appears before him.',
  'A ladder of remedies for sleepiness: change the perception; reflect on the teaching; recite it; pull your earlobes and rub your limbs; get up, wash your eyes, and look at the stars; focus on the perception of light; walk up and down with the senses turned inward. If all else fails, lie down mindfully in the lion\'s posture, resolving to get up. Then advice on not entering family quarrels and on speech, and finally the teaching on the ending of craving.',
  ['Eight remedies for drowsiness, in order.', 'Lie down only mindfully, with the intention to rise.'],
  ['drowsiness', 'meditation', 'walking'], ['an5.29', 'sn46.55']),
# ---------------- protection ----------------
'kp6': S('The Jewel Discourse', 'Ratana Sutta · රතන සූත්‍රය', 'The Khuddakapāṭha and Sutta Nipāta. The commentary sets it at Vesālī, struck by drought, famine, plague and spirits; Ānanda walked the city at night reciting it and sprinkling water from the Buddha\'s bowl.',
  'Addressed to all beings gathered there, earthly and heavenly: be kind to the humans who make offerings to you day and night. Then a series of truths about the three jewels, each ending "by this truth may there be wellbeing": the Buddha is supreme, the Dhamma\'s nibbāna and its concentration are unequalled, the noble persons are like an Indra pillar unshaken by the winds.',
  ['An act of truth (*saccakiriyā*).', 'The qualities of the Buddha, Dhamma and Saṅgha as protection.', 'The core of the Maha Pirith.'],
  ['paritta', 'protection', 'triple gem'], ['kp5', 'snp1.8', 'sn11.3']),
'an4.67': S('The King of Snakes', 'Ahirāja Sutta · Ahimetta Sutta', 'Sāvatthī. A monk has died of a snake bite.',
  'Had he spread loving-kindness to the four royal families of snakes, he would not have died. The Buddha gives the protective verses, the **Khandha paritta**: love for the footless, the two-footed, the four-footed and the many-footed; may none of them harm me; may all beings meet with good fortune. "Measureless is the Buddha, the Dhamma, the Saṅgha; limited are creeping things."',
  ['Mettā toward dangerous creatures.', 'The Khandha paritta, chanted for protection from snakes and harm.'],
  ['paritta', 'mettā', 'protection'], ['sn20.3', 'snp1.8']),
'sn46.14': S('Ill (1): Mahākassapa', 'Gilāna Sutta (1) · Bojjhaṅga', 'Rājagaha. Venerable Mahākassapa is gravely ill.',
  'The Buddha recites the **seven factors of awakening**: mindfulness, investigation of dhammas, energy, rapture, tranquillity, concentration and equanimity. Mahākassapa rejoices, and recovers. With SN 46.15 and 46.16, this is the root of the **Bojjhaṅga paritta**, chanted for the sick.',
  ['The seven awakening factors as healing.', 'Joy in the Dhamma as medicine.'],
  ['paritta', 'illness', 'awakening factors'], ['sn46.16', 'an10.60']),
'sn46.16': S('Ill (3): The Buddha', 'Gilāna Sutta (3)', 'Rājagaha. The Buddha himself is ill.',
  'Venerable Mahācunda recites the seven factors of awakening back to the Buddha, and the Buddha recovers. The teaching heals even its teacher.',
  ['The awakening factors, recited to the Buddha.'], ['paritta', 'illness'], ['sn46.14']),
}

# Visuddhimagga chapters: BJT Pali and Sinhala only (there is no public-domain English translation).
VM = {
'vm3': ('anya-vm-3', S('Taking Up a Meditation Subject', 'Visuddhimagga III · Kammaṭṭhānaggahaṇa Niddesa', 'Buddhaghosa\'s Path of Purification, written at the Mahāvihāra in Anurādhapura in the fifth century.',
  'How to begin. Cut the ten impediments; find a good friend (*kalyāṇamitta*) who can teach; know your temperament (greedy, hateful, deluded, faithful, intelligent, speculative); learn which of the **forty meditation subjects** suits it; and find a suitable place to live, avoiding the eighteen faults of a monastery.',
  ['The ten impediments (*palibodha*).', 'The good friend and how to approach a teacher.', 'Six temperaments and how to recognise them.', 'The forty subjects: ten kasiṇas, ten foulnesses, ten recollections, four divine abodes, four immaterial states, one perception, one defining.'],
  ['meditation', 'kammaṭṭhāna', 'teacher'], ['vm4', 'mn77'])),
'vm4': ('anya-vm-4', S('The Earth Kasiṇa', 'Visuddhimagga IV · Pathavīkasiṇa Niddesa', 'The Path of Purification, chapter four.',
  'The full manual for the earth kasiṇa: how to make the disk (dawn-coloured clay, a span and four fingers across), how to sit and gaze, the **learning sign** (*uggaha nimitta*) and the **counterpart sign** (*paṭibhāga nimitta*), protecting the sign, the ten skills in absorption, balancing the faculties, and the first through fifth jhānas.',
  ['Making and gazing at the kasiṇa.', 'From the learning sign to the counterpart sign.', 'Access and absorption concentration.', 'Ten kinds of skill in absorption; balancing faith with wisdom, energy with concentration.', 'The jhāna factors.'],
  ['kasiṇa', 'jhāna', 'meditation'], ['an10.25', 'vm5'])),
'vm5': ('anya-vm-5', S('The Remaining Kasiṇas', 'Visuddhimagga V · Sesakasiṇa Niddesa', 'The Path of Purification, chapter five.',
  'Water, **fire**, air, blue, yellow, red, white, light and limited space: how each kasiṇa is made or found, how its sign appears, and the particular powers each supports.',
  ['The fire kasiṇa: a screen with a hole before a fire of wood.', 'Colour kasiṇas from flowers or cloth.', 'General notes on all ten.'],
  ['kasiṇa', 'meditation'], ['vm4', 'an10.25'])),
'vm6': ('anya-vm-6', S('Foulness as a Meditation Subject', 'Visuddhimagga VI · Asubhakammaṭṭhāna Niddesa', 'The Path of Purification, chapter six.',
  'The ten kinds of corpse, from the bloated to the skeleton, as subjects for the greedy temperament: how to go to a charnel ground safely, how to note the surroundings, and how the sign arises. Taken up wisely, foulness meditation cools lust as cold water cools a fever.',
  ['Ten foulnesses (*dasa asubha*).', 'Practical cautions for the charnel ground.', 'Suitable for the greedy temperament.'],
  ['asubha', 'meditation', 'body'], ['snp1.11', 'mn10'])),
'vm7': ('anya-vm-7', S('The Six Recollections', 'Visuddhimagga VII · Cha-anussati Niddesa', 'The Path of Purification, chapter seven.',
  'The classical explanation, word by word, of *iti pi so bhagavā arahaṃ sammāsambuddho…*: the nine qualities of the Buddha, the six of the Dhamma, the nine of the Saṅgha; then recollection of virtue, generosity and the deities. The essential commentary for anyone who chants the Buddhānussati daily.',
  ['Each epithet of the Buddha explained.', 'Recollection leads to access concentration, joy and fearlessness.', 'One who practises it "comes to feel as if living in the Teacher\'s presence."'],
  ['buddhānussati', 'recollection', 'meditation'], ['an6.10', 'sn11.3'])),
'vm8a': ('anya-vm-8-1', S('Mindfulness of Death', 'Visuddhimagga VIII · Maraṇassati Kathā', 'The Path of Purification, chapter eight.',
  'Eight ways to recollect death: as a murderer with a raised sword; as the ruin of success; by comparison with the great who died; as the body shared with countless beings; as the frailty of life; as signless (no fixed time or place); as the limited length of life; as the shortness of the moment.',
  ['Eight modes of maraṇasati.', 'Practise with wise attention, without sorrow or fright.'],
  ['maraṇasati', 'meditation'], ['an6.19', 'an6.20'])),
'vm8b': ('anya-vm-8-2', S('Mindfulness of the Body', 'Visuddhimagga VIII · Kāyagatāsati Kathā', 'The Path of Purification, chapter eight.',
  'The thirty-two parts of the body: the sevenfold skill in learning them (verbal, mental, colour, shape, direction, location, delimitation) and the tenfold skill in attention, with the classical pentads beginning *kesā lomā nakhā dantā taco*.',
  ['The thirty-two parts.', 'Recitation forward and backward.', 'Colour, shape, location and boundaries of each part.'],
  ['body', 'asubha', 'meditation'], ['mn119', 'snp1.11'])),
'vm8d': ('anya-vm-8-4', S('Mindfulness of Breathing', 'Visuddhimagga VIII · Ānāpānassati Kathā', 'The Path of Purification, chapter eight.',
  'The classical commentary on the sixteen steps: counting, connecting, touching and fixing; the simile of the gatekeeper who watches only the gate; the sign that appears like a star, a cotton tuft or a breeze; and how the practice moves from calm to insight.',
  ['Counting (*gaṇanā*), following, contact, fixing.', 'The sawyer and gatekeeper similes.', 'The breath sign (*nimitta*).', 'All sixteen steps explained.'],
  ['ānāpānasati', 'meditation'], ['mn118', 'sn54.1'])),
'vm9': ('anya-vm-9', S('The Divine Abodes', 'Visuddhimagga IX · Brahmavihāra Niddesa', 'The Path of Purification, chapter nine.',
  'The classical manual for loving-kindness, compassion, gladness and equanimity. Begin with yourself, then a dear friend, a neutral person, and a hostile one; the ten methods for dissolving resentment; breaking down the barriers between self and others; and the near and far enemies of each abode.',
  ['Order of persons: self, the respected, the dear, the neutral, the hostile.', 'Ten ways to remove resentment.', 'Near enemies: lust for mettā, grief for compassion, excitement for gladness, indifference for equanimity.', 'The eleven benefits.'],
  ['mettā', 'brahmavihāra', 'meditation'], ['snp1.8', 'an11.15'])),
}
